{
  "rules": [
    "When recent history shows a sharp spike followed by immediate volatility (e.g., Example 1 and 2), the model's linear upward forecast is systematically biased; apply a mean-reversion correction factor of -5% to -8% against the first true outcome.",
    "If sentiment history contains mixed signals (positive and negative) within the last 7 days, the model's smooth trend ignores regime shifts; reduce forecast volatility by 40% and flatten the slope immediately after any single-day sentiment spike.",
    "In low-sentiment environments (all zeros or near-zero), the model overestimates momentum during consolidation phases; apply a downward bias correction of -2.5 EUR per day for the first 10 days of the forecast horizon.",
    "When true outcomes exhibit high-frequency oscillations (range >10 EUR within 3 days) despite low sentiment, the model's linear interpolation fails; switch to a range-bound strategy with a maximum deviation cap of ±3% from the initial price rather than projecting a trend."
  ]
}
